WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.251 --> 00:00:05.877
It's become strangely normal

00:00:05.877 --> 00:00:08.922
to tell our kids that they're going
to die from climate change.

00:00:08.964 --> 00:00:11.883
If sea-level rise doesn’t get them,
then a wildfire will,

00:00:11.925 --> 00:00:14.594
or a global famine, or crop failures.

00:00:14.594 --> 00:00:16.263
Maybe a fatal heat wave,

00:00:16.304 --> 00:00:17.639
the insect apocalypse

00:00:17.639 --> 00:00:19.558
or the fishless oceans.

00:00:19.558 --> 00:00:20.767
These are the headlines

00:00:20.767 --> 00:00:23.186
we've all been told will be
the end of humanity.

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:25.730
So we shouldn't be surprised, then,

00:00:25.730 --> 00:00:28.441
that young people today
feel crippled with anxiety.

00:00:28.441 --> 00:00:31.278
A large international survey
asked 10,000 young people

00:00:31.319 --> 00:00:33.989
about their attitudes to climate change.

00:00:34.030 --> 00:00:36.866
More than half said
they think humanity’s doomed;

00:00:36.908 --> 00:00:39.536
three-quarters find
the future frightening;

00:00:39.578 --> 00:00:43.665
and more than one in three are hesitant
to have children of their own.

00:00:43.665 --> 00:00:47.502
Young people today truly feel
like they could be the last generation.

00:00:47.544 --> 00:00:51.047
In fact, prominent activist groups
actually take on this very name.

00:00:51.715 --> 00:00:53.383
Now I get this feeling,

00:00:53.425 --> 00:00:54.884
I've been there.

00:00:54.884 --> 00:00:57.262
I used to feel like humanity was doomed,

00:00:57.304 --> 00:01:00.181
and despite having multiple
environmental degrees,

00:01:00.223 --> 00:01:03.268
I felt completely helpless
to do anything about it.

00:01:03.977 --> 00:01:05.520
But I'm a data scientist,

00:01:05.562 --> 00:01:09.608
and after years poring over the data
on how far humanity's come

00:01:09.608 --> 00:01:11.568
and how quickly things are now moving,

00:01:11.568 --> 00:01:13.862
my perspective on this has changed.

00:01:13.862 --> 00:01:16.740
I think we've got
this framing upside down.

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:18.742
Far from being the last generation,

00:01:18.783 --> 00:01:20.744
I think we will be the first generation:

00:01:20.785 --> 00:01:23.371
the first generation to be sustainable.

00:01:23.747 --> 00:01:25.498
When we think about sustainability,

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:29.711
we might imagine humans have only become
unsustainable in the very recent past,

00:01:29.753 --> 00:01:32.756
that our ancestors lived
in perfect balance with nature

00:01:32.797 --> 00:01:35.342
and only recently
has that been knocked off.

00:01:35.675 --> 00:01:37.802
Unfortunately, this is not true.

00:01:38.136 --> 00:01:41.431
If we're nostalgic for sustainable
paths to rewind back to,

00:01:41.473 --> 00:01:42.599
there is none.

00:01:43.266 --> 00:01:45.727
The world has never been sustainable.

00:01:46.478 --> 00:01:48.730
Now I get that that's
a controversial statement,

00:01:48.772 --> 00:01:50.148
so let me explain why.

00:01:50.190 --> 00:01:52.734
When we think about
the definition of sustainability,

00:01:52.776 --> 00:01:55.236
we might imagine something like this:

00:01:55.278 --> 00:01:59.199
"having a low environmental impact
to protect future generations."

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:01.409
And it's true, by this definition,

00:02:01.451 --> 00:02:03.745
many of our ancestors were sustainable.

00:02:03.787 --> 00:02:06.498
They did have a low environmental impact.

00:02:06.498 --> 00:02:09.000
But the reason they had
a low environmental impact

00:02:09.042 --> 00:02:11.378
is because the populations were tiny.

00:02:11.711 --> 00:02:13.922
And the reason their populations were tiny

00:02:13.963 --> 00:02:17.550
is because half of children died
before reaching puberty.

00:02:18.093 --> 00:02:19.761
Half of children died.

00:02:20.512 --> 00:02:22.222
That, then, raises the question:

00:02:22.222 --> 00:02:24.933
Is that what we really think
sustainability is?

00:02:24.974 --> 00:02:27.394
Is that the world we want
to maintain and preserve,

00:02:27.394 --> 00:02:29.729
one where half of our children die?

00:02:30.522 --> 00:02:32.315
Hope your answer to that is no.

00:02:32.357 --> 00:02:33.983
If we care about human suffering,

00:02:33.983 --> 00:02:36.486
we need to add another dimension to this.

00:02:36.486 --> 00:02:39.531
We need to also provide a good life
for everyone today.

00:02:40.198 --> 00:02:42.742
Now that gives our definition two halves.

00:02:42.784 --> 00:02:46.663
If we fail on either half,
we have failed to be sustainable.

00:02:46.705 --> 00:02:49.082
That's where our ancestors
were never sustainable.

00:02:49.124 --> 00:02:51.668
They never achieved
the first half of the equation.

00:02:52.544 --> 00:02:54.129
And over the last few centuries,

00:02:54.129 --> 00:02:57.090
the world has made
amazing progress on the first half.

00:02:57.132 --> 00:02:58.925
As we've shown at Our World in Data,

00:02:58.925 --> 00:03:01.052
child mortality is now down
to four percent.

00:03:01.094 --> 00:03:06.099
Extreme poverty used to be the default,
now less than one in 10 people live there.

00:03:06.141 --> 00:03:08.393
Literacy and education used to be rare;

00:03:08.393 --> 00:03:11.938
now, most children in the world
get the opportunity to go to school.

00:03:12.522 --> 00:03:15.567
But the world as it is today
is still unacceptable.

00:03:15.608 --> 00:03:18.445
It's unacceptable that millions
of children die every year,

00:03:18.486 --> 00:03:21.990
that hundreds of millions live
on less than two dollars a day,

00:03:21.990 --> 00:03:24.659
which is an incredibly low poverty line.

00:03:24.701 --> 00:03:27.078
But pick almost any metric
of human wellbeing,

00:03:27.078 --> 00:03:30.206
and you'll find that the world
has become a much better place.

00:03:30.790 --> 00:03:33.001
But that progress has come at a cost.

00:03:33.042 --> 00:03:35.503
It's come at a cost to the environment.

00:03:35.545 --> 00:03:38.006
We burn wood and fossil fuels for energy.

00:03:38.006 --> 00:03:40.759
We expanded farmlands
at the cost of forests,

00:03:40.759 --> 00:03:42.677
and our insatiable appetite for meat

00:03:42.677 --> 00:03:45.930
means we now use half
of the world's habitable land for farming.

00:03:45.972 --> 00:03:48.808
We kill billions of animals every year.

00:03:48.850 --> 00:03:51.060
These are the trends
that make us feel doomed.

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:55.231
We see these lines rising,
and we assume they might never stop.

00:03:55.273 --> 00:03:58.401
But more recent data tells us
a slightly different story.

00:03:58.443 --> 00:04:01.196
A more hopeful story,
that we can turn things around.

00:04:01.905 --> 00:04:06.701
This is per-capita CO2 emissions
in the UK since 1750.

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:10.747
Over the last few decades,
emissions in the UK have halved.

00:04:10.789 --> 00:04:12.916
That means that my carbon footprint today

00:04:12.957 --> 00:04:16.461
is less than half that of my grandparents'
when they were my age.

00:04:16.503 --> 00:04:19.672
That's despite the fact
I live a much more extravagant lifestyle,

00:04:19.714 --> 00:04:20.840
or, as they'd put it,

00:04:20.882 --> 00:04:24.093
"You youngsters just don't know
how good you've got it these days."

00:04:24.135 --> 00:04:25.136
(Laughter)

00:04:25.178 --> 00:04:27.388
Now, you might think
the UK's cheating here.

00:04:27.388 --> 00:04:29.808
It used to be this industrial powerhouse,

00:04:29.808 --> 00:04:33.603
now it gets China, India and Bangladesh
to produce its stuff for it.

00:04:33.603 --> 00:04:36.564
Maybe it's just offshored
all these emissions.

00:04:36.564 --> 00:04:38.358
There's a bit of truth to this --

00:04:38.399 --> 00:04:42.111
when we adjust for trade,
emissions in the UK are higher --

00:04:42.153 --> 00:04:45.907
but we still see this dramatic decline
over the last few decades.

00:04:45.949 --> 00:04:49.744
Offshoring is a bit of the story,
but it's not the entire story.

00:04:50.495 --> 00:04:53.665
At the same time,
the UK has increased its GDP.

00:04:53.706 --> 00:04:56.876
GDP has gone up,
while emissions have come down.

00:04:56.918 --> 00:04:59.087
And it's not the only country
to achieve this.

00:04:59.128 --> 00:05:01.923
You will see the same
for the US, for Germany,

00:05:01.965 --> 00:05:03.883
France, Spain, Portugal.

00:05:03.883 --> 00:05:06.845
A long list of countries
have increased GDP

00:05:06.845 --> 00:05:08.680
while reducing their emissions.

00:05:08.721 --> 00:05:12.100
And again, this is not just
because they've offshored them.

00:05:12.892 --> 00:05:14.561
The notion that economic growth

00:05:14.602 --> 00:05:17.564
has to be incompatible with reducing
our environmental impact

00:05:17.564 --> 00:05:18.857
is simply wrong.

00:05:19.774 --> 00:05:22.068
Now rich countries
are reducing their emissions,

00:05:22.110 --> 00:05:24.904
but low- and middle-income countries
are increasing theirs.

00:05:24.946 --> 00:05:27.115
What does this mean at a global level?

00:05:27.115 --> 00:05:31.077
Well, total CO2 emissions
are now beginning to flatline,

00:05:31.119 --> 00:05:34.956
but actually, emissions per person
already peaked a decade ago.

00:05:34.998 --> 00:05:38.084
That means the emissions
of the average person in the world today

00:05:38.084 --> 00:05:39.669
have peaked, and are now falling.

00:05:39.669 --> 00:05:42.297
And we will see a peak
in total CO2 emissions soon.

00:05:43.006 --> 00:05:44.424
Now, why is this happening?

00:05:44.465 --> 00:05:47.051
A big driver has been
technological change.

00:05:47.093 --> 00:05:50.346
Here, we see coal production
in the UK since 1700.

00:05:50.722 --> 00:05:53.224
Now the UK was the birthplace
of industrial coal,

00:05:53.266 --> 00:05:54.809
and it has now died there.

00:05:55.310 --> 00:05:56.477
When I was born,

00:05:56.519 --> 00:05:59.480
more than half of the UK's electricity
was coming from coal.

00:05:59.522 --> 00:06:01.774
This is now less than two percent.

00:06:01.816 --> 00:06:04.027
And it’s dying
in many other countries too.

00:06:04.068 --> 00:06:07.780
You will see the same
for the US, Germany, Denmark, Spain,

00:06:07.780 --> 00:06:09.866
Portugal, Greece, Ireland.

00:06:09.866 --> 00:06:12.869
In many countries in the world,
coal is dying.

00:06:13.536 --> 00:06:17.081
Taking its place are renewables,
where costs are plummeting.

00:06:17.373 --> 00:06:22.003
The cost of solar has fallen
by 99.8 percent since 1970,

00:06:22.045 --> 00:06:24.923
fallen by 90 percent
in the last decade alone.

00:06:25.381 --> 00:06:29.093
And if you're worried that it looks
like this price trend is leveling off,

00:06:29.093 --> 00:06:30.261
don't be.

00:06:30.303 --> 00:06:33.973
When we zoom in, we see
that the price of solar continues to fall.

00:06:34.015 --> 00:06:35.975
And the same is true for wind.

00:06:36.017 --> 00:06:37.435
Go back a decade,

00:06:37.435 --> 00:06:41.564
and solar and wind were among the most
expensive energy technologies we had.

00:06:41.606 --> 00:06:43.858
That is why the world
was not making progress,

00:06:43.858 --> 00:06:46.152
that is why countries
were not deploying them --

00:06:46.152 --> 00:06:47.737
they were far too expensive.

00:06:47.779 --> 00:06:50.698
But just ten years on,
and that script has flipped.

00:06:50.740 --> 00:06:54.661
In many countries in the world,
solar and wind are now the cheapest.

00:06:55.203 --> 00:06:58.790
Now if we're going to have renewables,
we're going to need energy storage.

00:06:58.790 --> 00:07:00.541
But there's good news there too.

00:07:00.541 --> 00:07:04.587
The price of batteries
has fallen by 98 percent since 1990.

00:07:05.088 --> 00:07:07.799
If you take the battery
you'd find in a Tesla today,

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:11.886
go back to 1990, it would have cost
one million dollars.

00:07:11.928 --> 00:07:13.930
It now costs just 12,000.

00:07:14.472 --> 00:07:17.225
That’s completely transformed
the world of energy storage

00:07:17.266 --> 00:07:20.019
and completely transformed
the world of transport.

00:07:20.436 --> 00:07:23.648
Global sales of petrol and diesel cars
have already peaked --

00:07:23.690 --> 00:07:26.818
they peaked in 2017,
and they are now falling.

00:07:27.193 --> 00:07:29.404
Taking their place are electric cars,

00:07:29.404 --> 00:07:33.157
where, in a space of just a few years,
sales are going through the roof.

00:07:34.325 --> 00:07:36.953
And from energy and transport
to the food we eat,

00:07:36.995 --> 00:07:39.080
you might imagine
that global deforestation

00:07:39.122 --> 00:07:40.790
is at its highest level ever.

00:07:40.832 --> 00:07:43.751
But actually, global deforestation
peaked decades ago

00:07:43.751 --> 00:07:45.545
and is now falling.

00:07:45.586 --> 00:07:47.547
But actually, it's better than that,

00:07:47.588 --> 00:07:50.842
because many countries
are now regrowing their old forests,

00:07:50.842 --> 00:07:54.095
such that the net decline
is even more impressive.

00:07:54.929 --> 00:07:56.472
Now, why is this happening?

00:07:56.514 --> 00:07:59.350
A big driver has been
increases in crop yields.

00:07:59.392 --> 00:08:03.688
For all of our agricultural history,
crop yields were low and stagnant.

00:08:03.730 --> 00:08:07.400
Farmers had basically
no opportunities to increase them.

00:08:07.442 --> 00:08:08.776
Over the last century,

00:08:08.818 --> 00:08:11.487
across many countries
and many different crop types,

00:08:11.529 --> 00:08:13.448
crop yields have skyrocketed.

00:08:13.781 --> 00:08:15.616
Here, we see it for the US, for corn,

00:08:15.616 --> 00:08:19.537
where yields have grown sixfold,
from two tonnes to 12 tonnes.

00:08:20.121 --> 00:08:22.665
Now what this means
is we can grow a lot more food

00:08:22.665 --> 00:08:24.083
from a lot less land.

00:08:24.083 --> 00:08:26.419
The amount of land
used to produce corn in the US

00:08:26.461 --> 00:08:28.963
has not increased in over a century,

00:08:29.005 --> 00:08:31.382
but look at the change in corn production.

00:08:32.717 --> 00:08:35.011
My main point here is that in the past,

00:08:35.011 --> 00:08:38.431
human progress had to come
at the cost of the environment.

00:08:38.473 --> 00:08:39.932
If we wanted energy,

00:08:39.932 --> 00:08:42.935
we had to burn wood
or we had to burn fossil fuels.

00:08:43.269 --> 00:08:44.771
If we wanted to grow more food,

00:08:44.771 --> 00:08:46.355
we had to expand farmland,

00:08:46.355 --> 00:08:48.316
often at the cost of forests.

00:08:48.983 --> 00:08:50.610
But technology and innovation

00:08:50.651 --> 00:08:53.154
means we're very quickly
decoupling these impacts,

00:08:53.196 --> 00:08:55.990
such that this conflict is no longer true.

00:08:57.116 --> 00:08:59.786
Let's then think about the world
that we can have.

00:08:59.827 --> 00:09:03.748
We need to achieve
the first half of our equation.

00:09:03.790 --> 00:09:05.792
We need to end global poverty.

00:09:05.792 --> 00:09:08.169
And here, I'm not talking
about raising everyone

00:09:08.169 --> 00:09:10.088
above a two-dollar-a-day poverty line.

00:09:10.088 --> 00:09:11.798
That is simply not good enough.

00:09:11.839 --> 00:09:14.801
We need to provide everyone
with a good, comfortable life.

00:09:15.676 --> 00:09:19.263
No child should die
from a cause that’s preventable.

00:09:19.305 --> 00:09:23.476
Every child should get the opportunity
to go to school and get an education.

00:09:24.435 --> 00:09:27.939
At the same time, we need to peak
and reduce our CO2 emissions;

00:09:27.980 --> 00:09:30.399
we need to move away from fossil fuels;

00:09:30.399 --> 00:09:32.026
and we need to end deforestation,

00:09:32.068 --> 00:09:36.114
while feeding eight, nine,
ten billion people, at the same time.

00:09:36.697 --> 00:09:37.949
How do we do that?

00:09:37.990 --> 00:09:40.034
How do we become the First Generation?

00:09:40.827 --> 00:09:44.705
First, our low-carbon technologies
need to become the default.

00:09:44.747 --> 00:09:46.541
There's two ways to do this.

00:09:46.541 --> 00:09:49.168
The first is to make sure
that they are affordable,

00:09:49.210 --> 00:09:51.420
to make sure that they are
the cheapest option.

00:09:51.921 --> 00:09:54.590
Solar and wind are already
cheaper than coal,

00:09:54.632 --> 00:09:57.510
electric cars will soon be
cheaper than gasoline,

00:09:57.510 --> 00:10:00.721
and alternative proteins
need to be cheaper than meat.

00:10:00.763 --> 00:10:02.223
The other way to do this

00:10:02.265 --> 00:10:04.809
is to make sure
these technologies are better.

00:10:04.809 --> 00:10:08.646
Environmental products have often been
promoted as this happy gimmick,

00:10:08.646 --> 00:10:10.523
but they need to go mainstream,

00:10:10.565 --> 00:10:14.068
and for that, they need to be better
than the high-carbon alternatives.

00:10:14.902 --> 00:10:17.405
Now my brother
recently got an electric car.

00:10:17.446 --> 00:10:18.865
He didn't get an electric car

00:10:18.906 --> 00:10:21.075
because he really cares
about the environment.

00:10:21.117 --> 00:10:23.369
He does not want to be
the next Greta Thunberg.

00:10:23.369 --> 00:10:25.872
And he definitely
does not want to be like me.

00:10:25.913 --> 00:10:27.957
(Laughter)

00:10:27.999 --> 00:10:31.294
He got an electric car because the design
and the driving experience

00:10:31.294 --> 00:10:34.005
were far better
than the petrol alternatives.

00:10:34.046 --> 00:10:37.091
That is how we make
low-carbon technologies the default.

00:10:37.967 --> 00:10:40.887
We need to reframe the way
we talk about sustainability

00:10:40.887 --> 00:10:43.222
as often promoted as a sacrifice.

00:10:43.264 --> 00:10:46.142
Environmental messaging
is often built on scarcity.

00:10:46.184 --> 00:10:48.811
It’s about reducing our lives
back to the bare minimum

00:10:48.853 --> 00:10:50.021
and no more.

00:10:50.021 --> 00:10:52.607
But this is not a vision
that inspires anyone.

00:10:52.648 --> 00:10:55.193
This is not the future we want to build.

00:10:55.193 --> 00:10:57.695
We need to reframe sustainability
as an opportunity,

00:10:57.737 --> 00:10:59.655
because it is an opportunity.

00:11:00.072 --> 00:11:03.743
It's an opportunity to provide clean,
abundant energy for everyone,

00:11:03.743 --> 00:11:05.995
whether that's powering
cities or countries,

00:11:06.037 --> 00:11:09.665
or getting rural communities connected
for the very first time.

00:11:10.291 --> 00:11:13.085
It’s about not being at the whims
of fossil-fuel markets

00:11:13.127 --> 00:11:15.421
or having millions
plunged into fuel poverty

00:11:15.421 --> 00:11:17.882
when dictators invade
neighboring countries.

00:11:18.466 --> 00:11:20.468
It's about breathing clean air.

00:11:20.509 --> 00:11:23.804
Here, we see the decline
in harmful air pollution in the US,

00:11:23.804 --> 00:11:26.933
from 2005 to 2021.

00:11:27.725 --> 00:11:30.770
Stopping people from dying
is not a sacrifice.

00:11:32.146 --> 00:11:35.107
Finally, it's a way to rethink
the way we live our lives,

00:11:35.149 --> 00:11:38.361
the way we design our cities
and our communities.

00:11:38.361 --> 00:11:41.239
Here, we see Copenhagen,
with bikes on the road,

00:11:41.239 --> 00:11:42.531
or Amsterdam,

00:11:42.531 --> 00:11:45.451
where you have
layer upon layer of bike rack.

00:11:45.451 --> 00:11:48.079
Now I'm not saying this is how
our cities have to look.

00:11:48.079 --> 00:11:50.331
We can design them in any way we want.

00:11:50.373 --> 00:11:53.376
What I'm saying is that sustainability
gives us the shelf

00:11:53.417 --> 00:11:55.544
to rethink the way we do things.

00:11:56.128 --> 00:11:59.382
Finally, we need to rethink
the way we use data.

00:11:59.423 --> 00:12:02.551
As a data scientist,
I take this very seriously.

00:12:02.593 --> 00:12:04.804
The environmentalists
have done an amazing job

00:12:04.845 --> 00:12:07.098
of waking the world up to these problems.

00:12:07.139 --> 00:12:10.226
Those charts of rising CO2
and rising temperature

00:12:10.226 --> 00:12:11.894
have been absolutely critical.

00:12:11.936 --> 00:12:13.813
They're why I'm on the stage today,

00:12:13.813 --> 00:12:16.399
they're why I got involved
in this in the first place.

00:12:17.024 --> 00:12:18.401
But there's a limit.

00:12:18.442 --> 00:12:21.028
When that's all we see,
we become paralyzed.

00:12:21.028 --> 00:12:22.363
We see these lines rising,

00:12:22.405 --> 00:12:24.657
and we assume that they might never stop.

00:12:24.657 --> 00:12:26.659
We assume that no progress has been made,

00:12:26.659 --> 00:12:28.869
that nothing good is happening.

00:12:28.869 --> 00:12:32.081
But as we've just seen,
this couldn't be further from the truth.

00:12:32.123 --> 00:12:34.250
We need to use data to inspire,

00:12:34.292 --> 00:12:35.876
to show what the problems are,

00:12:35.876 --> 00:12:37.503
also what the solutions are,

00:12:37.545 --> 00:12:39.547
and we need to show real signs of progress

00:12:39.588 --> 00:12:41.924
from countries, individuals, companies.

00:12:43.050 --> 00:12:46.846
Historically, our sustainability
equation went like this.

00:12:46.887 --> 00:12:48.472
It was one or the other.

00:12:48.514 --> 00:12:51.100
You could not have both at the same time.

00:12:51.142 --> 00:12:54.520
We can be the first generation
that does achieve both.

00:12:54.854 --> 00:12:56.939
Now the emphasis here is on "can."

00:12:56.939 --> 00:12:58.733
None of this is inevitable.

00:12:58.774 --> 00:12:59.984
It's not even inevitable

00:12:59.984 --> 00:13:02.528
that we'd have this opportunity
in the first place.

00:13:02.570 --> 00:13:04.905
We're only here
because of the relentless work

00:13:04.905 --> 00:13:09.076
of environmentalists, activists,
scientists, engineers,

00:13:09.076 --> 00:13:11.162
entrepreneurs, communicators,

00:13:11.203 --> 00:13:14.165
determined to make
the world a better place.

00:13:14.165 --> 00:13:17.626
They have brought us here,
and we need to take that forward.

00:13:17.626 --> 00:13:18.878
We need to do it bigger,

00:13:18.878 --> 00:13:20.963
and we need to do it much, much faster.

00:13:21.630 --> 00:13:23.632
But we do have the opportunity

00:13:23.674 --> 00:13:26.552
to be the first generation
that builds a sustainable world.

00:13:26.594 --> 00:13:27.762
Let's take it.

00:13:27.762 --> 00:13:28.929
Thank you.

00:13:28.971 --> 00:13:35.936
(Cheers and applause)

